# Partial logic

Claims are written in natural language first. Some of them are then also
written as an **atom**: a predicate applied to named things, in a fixed
spelling, so that two cards saying the same thing say it in the same string.

```
requires(strange-loop, level-crossing)
```

The concept is defined by the card `atom` in the knowledge base, and it is
wider than this spelling. Every claim is an atom: a statement taken whole and
given a value. The fixed spelling is an **atomic formula**, the same atom with
its inside made visible as a predicate and two arguments. The field named
`atom` in a claim holds the atomic formula, and in this folder "atom" on its
own means that.

The logic is partial in two ways, and both are intended.

## Partial in coverage

Only a claim that fits a predicate without distortion gets an atom. The rest
stay in natural language, and that is not a defect to be worked off. A claim
such as "the seven definitions are not rival answers to one question" has no
honest rendering in fourteen predicates, and forcing one would put a false
precision into every number computed from it. About two thirds of the claims
carry an atom.

What is formalised is what recurs. A predicate earns its place by being needed
on many cards, and a thing earns a fixed name by being spoken of from several
sides. The query [recurring terms](../.hql/queries/recurring-terms.hql) counts
both.

## Partial in truth

No card decides an atom for the vault. A card asserts an atom, denies it, or
says nothing about it. Collected over all cards, each atom therefore has one of
four statuses.

| Status | Meaning | Listed as |
|---|---|---|
| asserted | some claim asserts it and none denies it | `asserted` |
| denied | some claim denies it and none asserts it | `denied` |
| unsaid | no claim speaks on it | not listed |
| contradicted | one claim asserts what another denies | `both` |

This is the four-valued scheme Belnap proposed for a reasoner whose
information comes from several sources that need not agree. Two properties of
it matter here.

- A contradiction stays where it is. An atom with the status `both` does not
  make every other atom derivable, as it would in classical logic. It is a
  located fact about two claims, with their addresses.
- Silence is not denial. A card that says nothing about an atom has not said
  it is false, and no score treats it as if it had.

The query [atom status](../.hql/queries/atom-status.hql) lists every atom with
its status and the claims on each side, contradictions first.

```hql
from claims import said

cards
| flat_map(said)
| filter(s => s.atom == "requires(strange-loop, level-crossing)")
| map(s => {claim: s.ref, asserts: s.asserts, belief: s.belief})
```

## Writing an atom

An atom is `predicate(first, second)`: lower case, one space after the comma.

An argument is the name of a card whenever the thing has a card or is linked
as one, and otherwise a short term in the same spelling, such as `base-case`.

A claim that denies something uses the positive atom and adds `holds: false`.
There are no negative predicates. "Abstraction is not summarization" is

```yaml
atom: same-as(abstraction, summarization)
holds: false
```

## The predicates

| Predicate | Reading |
|---|---|
| `is-a(x, y)` | every x is a y |
| `instance-of(x, y)` | x is one member of the class y |
| `part-of(x, y)` | x is a component or step of y |
| `example-of(x, y)` | x is an example of y |
| `same-as(x, y)` | x and y are the same concept or operation |
| `requires(x, y)` | x cannot be or happen without y |
| `suffices-for(x, y)` | having x is enough for y |
| `produces(x, y)` | x yields y as its result |
| `uses(x, y)` | x relies on y |
| `precedes(x, y)` | x comes before y |
| `generalizes(x, y)` | x is a wider form of y |
| `analogous-to(x, y)` | x and y correspond in structure |
| `inverse-of(x, y)` | x undoes or runs opposite to y |
| `has(x, p)` | x has the property p |

`same-as`, `analogous-to` and `inverse-of` are symmetric, and their arguments
are written in alphabetical order. Without that rule `same-as(a, b)` and
`same-as(b, a)` would be two atoms and the cards on either side would never be
compared.

Several predicates repeat a relation kind of the links (`is-a`, `part-of`,
`uses`). A link says that two cards are connected and how. An atom on a claim
says the same with a probability attached and with a card answerable for it,
and it can be denied, which a link cannot.

## What the logic does not do

No query derives anything. The statuses above compare atoms only when they
are the same string: if one card asserts `is-a(a, b)` and another
`is-a(b, c)`, no status records `is-a(a, c)`.

Derivation is a separate step, described in [derivation](derivation.hmd). A
short list of rules over the atoms is applied by a script, and an atom that
is derived from some claims and denied by another is a contradiction no
comparison of strings could find.

The rules for deriving along one predicate, and their proofs, are in
[from distance to logic](distance-to-logic.hmd).
